          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The consensus treats the tree as a polyvalent image whose broad positive center is life and flourishing under God, especially a person or community whose character is known by its fruit. It also recognizes distinct uses for rulers, kingdoms, Israel, the kingdom of God, the Tree of Life, the cross, and judgment.\",\"book_summary\":\"The book gives these personal and corporate uses a unified covenantal structure: a rooted, fruit-bearing life can grow from one person or seed into a household, lineage, people, or nation, with branches as members and fruit enabling further growth.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"A living person or community rooted in God, flourishing and manifesting its spiritual or moral condition through fruit.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"An organic covenant person-or-people whose rooted life is known by fruit and expands genealogically or corporately from an individual seed into a larger body.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_NARROWS\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book narrows the broad life-and-flourishing image to covenantal, reproductive, and corporate growth. It also reclassifies several consensus cases—individuals, rulers, Israel, nations, and the growing kingdom—as different scales or parts of one organic covenant pattern. Major consensus senses such as the Tree of Life, the cross, forbidden knowledge, idolatry, and trees chiefly signifying judgment are not included in the entry's stated identification.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no direct contradiction at the central referent: both identify trees with persons or peoples whose nature is displayed in fruit. The tension is that the consensus regards many tree images as contextually distinct, whereas the book organizes several of them into a single covenant-growth model.\",\"rationale\":\"Psalm 1 and Luke 6 directly support rooted or fruitful persons known by their fruit. Daniel 4 supports a person or ruler represented by a tree; Matthew 13 supports growth from seed into a sheltering tree; Ezekiel 17 supports planted growth, branches, fruit, and shelter; and Romans 11 supports branches as members of a covenant people. Isaiah 51 supplies the one-person-to-many pattern through Abraham, though it does not itself use tree imagery. Thus the book retains the consensus core but adds a materially more precise account of covenant scale, membership, and propagation.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The entry may conflate metaphors that the cited contexts use for different immediate referents—a righteous individual, Abrahamic multiplication, a restored kingdom, Nebuchadnezzar's dominion, the kingdom of heaven, moral character, and covenant inclusion. The specific claims that fruit becomes seed and that every tree develops through a person-house-lineage-nation sequence are not directly established by the printed excerpts.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
